.TH EUCLID-CLI-ETS-GET-SERVER 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ets-get-server \- show one transfer server's definition
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ets get-server
.BR \-\-server\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows one transfer server's full definition as JSON: its protocol,
address and port, the ERN and name of the bucket it fronts, the EAM
users and groups allowed to log in, the SSH host key or passive port
range as applicable, and both its desired and observed state.
.PP
This is
.BR euclid-cli-ets-list-servers (1)
narrowed to a single server, and reports the same two states - see that
page for what a persistent disagreement between them means.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-server\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the server to show. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The server was found; its definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, the server could not be reached, or no server has that ID
(HTTP 404).
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets get-server \e
    --server-id sftp1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check which users a server currently admits, without pretty-printing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 --pretty false \e
    ets get-server --server-id sftp1
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-list-servers (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-update-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-delete-server (1)
